WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840
When users interact with a premium iOS application,

2
00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:09.840
they expect an uninterrupted, perfectly synchronized 60 to 120 frames per second.

3
00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.240
Visual consistency is the absolute baseline for native development.

4
00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:16.640
But historically in React native,

5
00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:20.880
those mechanisms could slip out of phase, resulting in UI tearing.

6
00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:24.480
You would see a frame where half of you updated to a new state,

7
00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:28.800
while the rest lagged behind, creating a visually broken experience.

8
00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:31.600
The root cause of this tearing was structural.

9
00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:35.680
React native state updates and native iOS UI animations

10
00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:38.320
were completely decoupled from one another.

11
00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.200
To render a single frame in the old architecture,

12
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:45.360
data had to travel across three isolated computing territories.

13
00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:49.040
First, the JavaScript thread, which described the component tree.

14
00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:52.560
Second, a background C++ shadow thread running yoga,

15
00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.240
which calculated the layout.

16
00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:58.320
And finally, the iOS main thread, which actually painted the UI.

17
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.680
This diagram shows the specific point of failure,

18
00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:04.320
the boundary between those territories.

19
00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:06.720
To pass data from one thread to the next,

20
00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:11.200
it had to be packed into an asynchronous serialized JSON bridge.

21
00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:15.360
For an iOS engineer, this created a persistent race condition.

22
00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:19.520
A user could touch the screen, and that touch event would arrive on the main thread

23
00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:22.960
while the view tree was still rendering a stale pre-update state

24
00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:24.720
stuck somewhere in the queue.

25
00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:28.240
Because every single boundary in this pipeline was asynchronous,

26
00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:30.480
guaranteeing that a React native view update

27
00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:34.720
and an animation frame committed in the exact same core animation transaction

28
00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:36.160
was mathematically impossible.

29
00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:40.400
React native's new architecture addresses this by entirely discarding

30
00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:44.160
the old rendering pipeline in favor of a system called fabric.

31
00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.960
The foundational thesis of fabric is the complete removal

32
00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:49.120
of the JSON serialization bridge.

33
00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:53.840
The bridge is replaced by a shared synchronous C++ shadow tree,

34
00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:57.280
which operates on top of the JavaScript interface, or JSI.

35
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:02.400
JSI gives both the JavaScript runtime and the iOS main thread

36
00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:06.400
the ability to read and write to the exact same memory space instantly.

37
00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:10.640
By tracing how this shared memory model executes a render frame,

38
00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:14.800
we can see exactly how fabric guarantees atomic UI updates

39
00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:17.680
and how developers map those updates to native elements

40
00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:19.840
using RCT view component view.

41
00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:23.120
To understand the efficiency of the new model,

42
00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:26.400
we have to look closely at the exact failure points of the old one.

43
00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:31.200
A legacy render begins when JavaScript produces a description of the component tree.

44
00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:33.760
This diagram maps the legacy pipeline.

45
00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.560
To cross into the background shadow thread,

46
00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:38.960
that component tree is translated into JSON.

47
00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:41.840
This is the first serialization step.

48
00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:43.920
Once it arrives in the background red,

49
00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:46.240
the yoga engine calculates the layout

50
00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:49.040
and generates a list of mutation operations,

51
00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:51.920
the specific instructions for what the UI needs to do,

52
00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.120
then comes the fatal second serialization.

53
00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.000
Those mutations are packed up again

54
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:01.280
and pushed asynchronously into a queue waiting for the main thread.

55
00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:05.680
The main thread has no idea a UI change has even been requested

56
00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:08.240
until every packet finishes traversing that queue.

57
00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:11.600
Engineers had to write workarounds to manage this delay,

58
00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:15.280
coordinating native animations with react transitions,

59
00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:18.000
required wrapping logic in interaction manager,

60
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:21.280
or forcing use native driver flags to bypass the congestion.

61
00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:25.040
As long as the rendering pipeline relied on a synchronous message

62
00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:28.480
passing, deterministic UI synchronization was unattainable.

63
00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:31.680
Fabric clears away those queues entirely.

64
00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:35.280
In the new architecture, the pipeline starts the same way.

65
00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:37.920
JavaScript calls react reconciliation,

66
00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:41.360
but as this diagram illustrates, there is no bridge.

67
00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:46.720
The C++ fabric renderer traverses the resulting element tree immediately.

68
00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:49.600
It builds out the core data structure of fabric,

69
00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:53.120
a tree made up of strongly typed shadow node objects,

70
00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:54.880
living strictly in C++.

71
00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:58.800
Every shadow node carries three precise pieces of data.

72
00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:01.680
The typed props coming from JavaScript,

73
00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:05.040
native state that the UI can update, and the layout results.

74
00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:08.240
Those layout results are calculated instantly

75
00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:11.760
because the yoga engine is now embedded directly inside fabric,

76
00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:13.760
operating synchronously on the nodes.

77
00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:16.480
Because these nodes are defined as strictly typed

78
00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:20.160
structs in C++, the payload is structurally safe.

79
00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:22.320
There are no loose JSON strings.

80
00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:24.160
The data is rigid and predictable.

81
00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:27.280
Fabric replaces asynchronous message passing

82
00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:29.040
with synchronous memory sharing,

83
00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:32.400
priming the exact data needed for an immediate UI kit update.

84
00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:34.720
Once the layout is calculated,

85
00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:38.160
control shifts strictly to the iOS main thread for the commit face.

86
00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:41.760
Fabric iterates over the C++ shadow node tree

87
00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:44.560
and compiles a definitive list of visual mutations.

88
00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:47.760
Every change is mapped to one of four mutation types.

89
00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:51.120
Create, insert, update, or delete.

90
00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:54.320
This diagram shows the exact handoff.

91
00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:58.480
The C++ data funnels directly into the iOS mounting coordinator,

92
00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:00.960
the engine responsible for actually touching the views.

93
00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:03.520
The coordinator then hands these instructions

94
00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:05.600
to RCT surface presenter.

95
00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:08.240
This is fabric central iOS coordinator.

96
00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:11.920
RCT surface presenter has one critical job.

97
00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:14.240
It takes every single view creation

98
00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:16.240
and property update in that frame

99
00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:19.440
and groups them tightly inside a single cat transaction.

100
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:23.600
By locking all mutations into a single cat transaction,

101
00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:27.360
Fabric executes the native equivalent of an atomic UI operation.

102
00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:31.040
Structurally, no view can present partially updated,

103
00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:33.120
effectively eliminating UI tearing.

104
00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:37.200
To see how developers actually hook into this atomic process,

105
00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:40.240
we have to look down at the individual iOS component level.

106
00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:45.200
The legacy RCT view manager and its objective C property export macros

107
00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:46.480
have been retired.

108
00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:49.760
The new foundational unit of work for mapping native UI

109
00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:51.680
is RCT view component view.

110
00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:53.840
As this diagram details,

111
00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:58.640
RCT view component view is an objective C++ subclass of UI view

112
00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:00.560
that implements a specific protocol

113
00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:04.480
to straddle the line between C++ logic and UI kit rendering.

114
00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:07.600
Its core inward responsibility is intercepting

115
00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:09.760
the strongly typed prop and state updates

116
00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:12.320
flowing directly from the C++ shadow tree.

117
00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:15.440
Its outward responsibility is executing callbacks,

118
00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:20.880
firing user interactions back up to JavaScript via a typed C++ event emitter.

119
00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:22.480
When building with this subclass,

120
00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:25.840
the architectural best practice is the Swift helper pattern.

121
00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:29.840
In this pattern, the actual drawing logic and UI kit gesture handling

122
00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:33.600
live entirely within a pure dedicated Swift UI view.

123
00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:37.040
The objective C++ RCT view component view

124
00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:39.120
acts merely as a lightweight delegate,

125
00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:41.280
doing nothing but receiving Fabric's data

126
00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:43.920
and instantly passing it down to the Swift view.

127
00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:47.600
This mirrors how standard reusable UI kit components are built.

128
00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:49.920
It keeps the Swift code entirely clean,

129
00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:51.280
independently testable,

130
00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:54.960
and isolated from react natives internal C++ engine.

131
00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:58.480
With the pipeline mapped from the JavaScript interface down to the Swift view,

132
00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:02.480
we can evaluate the final performance profile of a completed render frame.

133
00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:05.600
Fabric provides a strict guarantee to custom components.

134
00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:09.680
The update props method is always called on the main queue during the commit phase.

135
00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:11.200
Because of this guarantee,

136
00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:15.760
engineers can apply core animation updates directly inside update props.

137
00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:19.680
There is absolutely no need to wrap view updates in Dispatch Async

138
00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:21.280
to reach the main thread.

139
00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:25.600
In fact, using DispatchQ.main.async inside these updates

140
00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:27.440
is now actively harmful.

141
00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:31.680
Deferring the execution pushes the update outside of Fabric's commit window,

142
00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:34.800
breaking the adamicity of the CA transaction.

143
00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:39.200
If you need to perfectly coordinate a layer animation with a react native transition,

144
00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:44.320
you handle it by calling CATransaction.begin and commit explicitly within update props.

145
00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:48.000
Look at the complete execution path in this diagram.

146
00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:52.320
A state update triggered from a Swift native module can now travel through JavaScript,

147
00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:55.280
calculate layout in the shared C++ shadow tree,

148
00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:58.080
and terminate in the main thread CA transaction,

149
00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:00.080
all within a single run loop tick.

150
00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:03.680
By moving layout calculation into synchronous C++,

151
00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:08.320
unlocking visual mutations inside explicit core animation transactions,

152
00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:10.880
Fabric natively bridges the performance gap,

153
00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:14.400
aligning react native directly with Apple's compositing model.

